  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h1>For tonight</h1>
      <p>This page was made by people who have had nights like this one. It is peer support, not medical advice, and it is not a crisis service. If you might act on what you're feeling, skip the rest of this page and call or text <a href="tel:988">988</a>, or text TALK to <a href="sms:741741">741741</a>, right now.</p>
      <h2>First: make the next hour safer</h2>
      <p>Put distance between you and anything you could use to hurt yourself. Another room. A locked drawer. A friend's house. Time and distance are not small things — they are the thing.</p>
      <p>Don't be alone with it if you can help it. Go where people are — a porch, a store that's open late, a roommate's doorway. If nobody is near, call the California Peer-Run Warm Line at <a href="tel:+18556009276">1-855-600-WARM (9276)</a>, or call or text 988 and just stay on the line.</p>
      <p>Skip alcohol and anything else tonight. They turn the volume up on exactly the feelings you're trying to survive.</p>
      <h2>Then: ten minutes at a time</h2>
      <p>You don't have to solve your life tonight. You only have to get through the next ten minutes. Then the next ten.</p>
      <p>Cold water on your face or hands for 30 seconds. Ice held in your hands works too. It gives your body something loud and harmless to pay attention to.</p>
      <p>Breathe out slower than you breathe in. In for four, out for six, a few rounds. The long exhale is the part that tells your body the emergency can stand down a little.</p>
      <p>Eat something small. Drink water. Low fuel makes everything feel more final than it is.</p>
      <h2>Say it out loud to someone</h2>
      <p>A text counts. "I'm not okay tonight" is a complete sentence. If you don't know how to start, <a href="/resources/how-to-tell-someone/">How to tell someone</a> has first lines you can borrow.</p>
      <h2>Plan for morning</h2>
      <p>Before you sleep, put one thing on tomorrow's list that is small and real: a shower, a call, a walk around the block. And if tonight gets worse instead of better, the plan is 988 — any hour, no explanation needed.</p>
      <p>You found this page at the right time: before, not after. Stay.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
